{"parentUuid":"f8386448-07a0-4659-be95-53114d0a49cd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a823b529b0879c4bb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BjWyrQL4gXAskp3pac5u6X","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SCIP Gurobi CPLEX warm-start dual bound serialization MIP restart 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Cplex - Gurobi - UL HPC Tutorials\",\"url\":\"https://ulhpc-tutorials.readthedocs.io/en/production/maths/Cplex-Gurobi/\"},{\"title\":\"🧠 MIP Solvers Unleashed: A Beginner’s Guide to PuLP, CPLEX, Gurobi, Google OR-Tools, and Pyomo | by Ashutosh Shenoy | Operations Research Bit | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/operations-research-bit/mip-solvers-unleashed-a-beginners-guide-to-pulp-cplex-gurobi-google-or-tools-and-pyomo-0150d4bd3999\"},{\"title\":\"LP Warm Starts - using Gurobi with JuMP - Optimization (Mathematical) - Julia Programming Language\",\"url\":\"https://discourse.julialang.org/t/lp-warm-starts-using-gurobi-with-jump/73840\"},{\"title\":\"Reuse a previously optimized model for warm start – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/31240690993553-Reuse-a-previously-optimized-model-for-warm-start\"},{\"title\":\"Why are MIP solvers like Gurobi, CPLEX, and SCIP implemented in C instead of C++? - Quora\",\"url\":\"https://www.quora.com/Why-are-MIP-solvers-like-Gurobi-CPLEX-and-SCIP-implemented-in-C-instead-of-C\"},{\"title\":\"An Efficient Local Search Solver for Mixed Integer Programming Peng Lin # Ñ\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol307-cp2024/LIPIcs.CP.2024.19/LIPIcs.CP.2024.19.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Warm-start basis for mip in root relaxation – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/21303851057041-Warm-start-basis-for-mip-in-root-relaxation\"},{\"title\":\"can ecole use cplex/gurobi as options for LP solvers during the Branching process? · Issue #258 · ds4dm/ecole\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ds4dm/ecole/issues/258\"},{\"title\":\"Cplex AdvInd (Advanced Start Switch) equivalent in Gurobi – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/360077078831-Cplex-AdvInd-Advanced-Start-Switch-equivalent-in-Gurobi\"},{\"title\":\"Collab-Solver: Collaborative Solving Policy Learning for Mixed-Integer Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2508.03030v2\"}]\n\nThe search results provide general information about MIP solvers (SCIP, Gurobi, CPLEX) and some warm-start capabilities, but don't contain specific recent research or developments on the combined topics of warm-start dual bound serialization and MIP restart features from 2024-2025. Here's what was found:\n\n**General Solver Information:**\nModern state-of-the-art MIP solvers are based on the branch-and-bound framework, including commercial solvers Gurobi and CPLEX, and academic solvers SCIP and HiGHS.\n\n**Warm-Start Capabilities:**\nProviding a start solution can generally speed up the Gurobi Optimizer, making subsequent optimization faster than the first solve, though this improvement is not guaranteed.\n\nA feature similar to CPLEX's AdvInd setting is on Gurobi's future roadmap and will be added in an upcoming release. The search results also mention that Gurobi version 9.5 introduced the parameter LPWarmStart to control this feature.\n\n**Limitations:**\nCommercial solvers CPLEX and Gurobi do not provide open APIs for custom cut selection and branching policies.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific recent research papers or documentation from 2024-2025 addressing the specific combination of warm-start dual bound serialization and MIP restart techniques across these solvers. For more targeted information, you may want to search for academic papers on optimization solver development from recent conferences or journals.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c91f1a87-145f-4c07-8b68-ad6bb072ca7f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:33.179Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f8386448-07a0-4659-be95-53114d0a49cd","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6f60ab09-331c-4cc6-8a90-1f551301bada","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a823b529b0879c4bb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D1vuRrKFTr7hh86UPsNwm6","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"anytime constraint programming Lecoutre Boussemart certificate epsilon optimal 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"dblp: Christophe Lecoutre\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/pid/95/2777.html\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"CP: Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=572\"},{\"title\":\"Reasoning from last conflict(s) in constraint programming - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0004370209001040\"},{\"title\":\"30th International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2024)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-307\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint Networks: Targeting Simplicity for Techniques and Algorithms: Lecoutre, Christophe: 9781848211063: Amazon.com: Books\",\"url\":\"https://www.amazon.com/Constraint-Networks-Simplicity-Techniques-Algorithms/dp/1848211066\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] XCSP3: An Integrated Format for Benchmarking Combinatorial Constrained Problems | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/XCSP3:-An-Integrated-Format-for-Benchmarking-Boussemart-Lecoutre/441e87ecb1f623cc3c57f076a99a7ffa010b1f14\"},{\"title\":\"Christophe Lecoutre\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=bFRpFuUAAAAJ&hl=en\"},{\"title\":\"MiniCP: a lightweight solver for constraint programming | Mathematical Programming Computation | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-020-00190-7\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint Networks: Techniques and Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/book/10.1002/9780470611821\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the authors and constraint programming research, but the specific terms \"certificate epsilon optimal\" were not directly mentioned in relation to anytime constraint programming in the search results.\n\nHere's what I found:\n\nChristophe Lecoutre is a Professor of Computer Science at CRIL, University of Artois, specializing in constraint programming and artificial intelligence. Frédéric Boussemart and Christophe Lecoutre are authors who have collaborated on XCSP3 and its ecosystem.\n\nThe search results show recent work by Lecoutre and colleagues in constraint programming for 2024-2025, including a 2025 CP conference paper by Lecoutre on \"Aircraft Resource-Constrained Assembly Line Balancing with Learning Effect: A Constraint Programming Approach\".\n\nRegarding anytime algorithms, the search results discuss various anytime extensions of A* that have been proposed to compute a sequence of paths of increasing quality until finding an optimal path and proving its optimality.\n\nHowever, the specific topic combining \"certificate epsilon optimal\" with anytime constraint programming wasn't found in these results. If you're looking for a specific paper or research on this topic, you might want to try a more targeted search or provide additional context about what you're looking for.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"95c296d0-2ed4-4c5d-9cad-dada14220fc2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:34.438Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6f60ab09-331c-4cc6-8a90-1f551301bada","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ee8dd2c3-8f45-427a-a63c-356b16c17b84","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a823b529b0879c4bb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RxYU9XetPYbNbd9e1HFUhv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"VeriPB DRAT pseudo-boolean optimality certificate 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Pseudo Boolean Competition 2025\",\"url\":\"http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/PB25/\"},{\"title\":\"Certified MaxSAT Preprocessing | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-63498-7_24\"},{\"title\":\"Certifying Pareto Optimality in Multi-objective Maximum Satisfiability | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-90653-4_6\"},{\"title\":\"Pseudo-Boolean Proof Logging for Optimal Classical Planning\",\"url\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/ICAPS/article/download/36101/38255/40174\"},{\"title\":\"MIAO / Software / VeriPB · GitLab\",\"url\":\"https://gitlab.com/MIAOresearch/software/VeriPB\"},{\"title\":\"VERIPB and CAKEPB in the SAT Competition 2023\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2024/downloads/checkers/veripb.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Certifying Parity Reasoning Eﬃciently Using Pseudo-Boolean Proofs∗\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.12185\"},{\"title\":\"Pseudo-Boolean Proof Logging for Optimal Classical Planning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2504.18443\"},{\"title\":\"Documentation of VeriPB and CakePB for the SAT Competition 2025 Markus Anders\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2025/downloads/checkers/veripb.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Formal Methods in Computer-Aided Design 2024 Translating Pseudo-Boolean Proofs\",\"url\":\"https://repositum.tuwien.at/bitstream/20.500.12708/200790/1/Nukala-2024-Translating%20Pseudo-Boolean%20Proofs%20into%20Boolean%20Clausal%20Proofs-vor.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about VeriPB, DRAT, and pseudo-Boolean optimality certificates in 2024-2025:\n\n## VeriPB Overview\n\nVeriPB is a proof checker for verifying pseudo-Boolean certificates of satisfiability, unsatisfiability, and optimality bounds. The VeriPB proof system is an extension of the cutting planes proof system.\n\n## Recent Developments (2024-2025)\n\nSeveral significant research projects have been published using VeriPB for optimality certification:\n\n1. **Certified MaxSAT Preprocessing**: Pseudo-Boolean proof logging can be used to certify the correctness of a wide range of modern MaxSAT preprocessing techniques.\n\n2. **Multi-objective MaxSAT**: VeriPB preorders can be used to provide certificates for MO-MaxSAT algorithms that compute a representative solution for each element of the non-dominated set.\n\n3. **Classical Planning**: VeriPB has been applied to optimization problems such as MaxSAT, ILP presolving, and constraint programming, with a recent paper on pseudo-Boolean proof logging for optimal classical planning published in 2025.\n\n## Relationship to DRAT\n\nThe pseudo-Boolean proof system subsumes DRAT, and pseudo-Boolean VeriPB proof logging produces linear-size proofs while DRAT produces quadratic-size proofs for certain reasoning tasks.\n\n## Competition Standards\n\nCertificates of unsatisfiability/optimality must be generated in the VeriPB format for the Pseudo Boolean Competition 2025.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"52662140-a566-473b-8247-c585858b3aca","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:34.601Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ee8dd2c3-8f45-427a-a63c-356b16c17b84","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7b9e2a3f-85e6-4a85-801a-c89bf8133aac","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a823b529b0879c4bb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XurSv1d7kebKy9e4CZ8DEs","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT OR-tools warm start hint cut pool reuse multi-instance 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"warm start cp-sat ortools\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/minizinc/c/1QRwbQhStHg\"},{\"title\":\"📈 #76 Optimizing with Google OR-Tools: strategies and insights from three years of real-world experience\",\"url\":\"https://www.feasible.club/p/76-optimizing-with-google-or-tools\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/releases\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Release Notes | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/support/release_notes\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about CP-SAT and OR-Tools regarding warm start, hints, cuts, and cut pool:\n\n## Warm Start and Hints\n\nIf you have a good intuition about how the solution might look, you can inform CP-SAT to incorporate this knowledge into its search, and some workers will try to follow these hints, which can significantly improve the solver's performance if they are good. The `add_hint()` method adds 'var == value' as a hint to the solver.\n\nHints must cover all variables they touch and remain feasible after presolve or they're ignored. CP-SAT prefers full hints, meaning all variables.\n\n## Cut Pool and Constraint Management\n\nCP-SAT starts with an empty LP and adds constraints from the pool in small batches. The solver generates cuts mainly at \"level zero\".\n\n## Recent Updates (2024-2025)\n\nThe release 9.12 in February added Python 3.13 support, revamped hint handling, and sped up the linear expression layer.\n\n## Multi-instance and Parallelization\n\nWhile the search results don't specifically address multi-instance execution details, the broader documentation indicates OR-Tools supports parallelization among other features.\n\nThe search results provide foundational information but don't contain detailed technical documentation specifically addressing multi-instance cut pool reuse strategies in recent 2024-2025 releases.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"bb8cf799-56f5-4571-af19-cf0288e519fb","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:39.882Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7b9e2a3f-85e6-4a85-801a-c89bf8133aac","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
