{"parentUuid":"b4f7980e-ab58-4b67-b0c7-913130fc54e1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a823b529b0879c4bb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016F6kuVndxK9mTxB9iR3NqN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"epsilon optimal\" certificate exploration certified MIP industrial 2024 2025 paper\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"WC266 — Certified Optimization - ISMP 2024\",\"url\":\"https://ismp2024.gerad.ca/schedule/WC/266\"},{\"title\":\"1 2025-2026 Master’s Degree in Industrial Property (MIP) Jointly Offered by\",\"url\":\"https://welc.wipo.int/uploads/MIP_TONGJI_2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MIP Test Planner 2024-25 - dropper neet (1) (1)\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/773721694/MIP-Test-Planner-2024-25-dropper-neet-1-1\"},{\"title\":\"IPM Answer Key 2024-2025 | PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/856858443/IPM-Answer-Key-2024-2025\"},{\"title\":\"Certifying MIP-Based Presolve Reductions for 0–1 Integer Linear Programs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.09277\"},{\"title\":\"ASE 2024 - Most Influential Papers (MIP) - Conferences\",\"url\":\"https://conf.researchr.org/track/ase-2024/ase-2024-most-influential-papers--mip-\"},{\"title\":\"IEEE ICIP 2024 || Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates || 27-30 October 2024\",\"url\":\"https://cmsworkshops.com/ICIP2024/papers/accepted_papers.php\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Verification of First-Order Methods via Mixed-Integer Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.11330v5\"},{\"title\":\"MIPcc25: The MIP Workshop 2025 Computational Competition | mixedinteger\",\"url\":\"https://www.mixedinteger.org/2025/competition/\"},{\"title\":\"Third Edition of the 2024-2025 Master's Degree in ...\",\"url\":\"https://welc.wipo.int/uploads/MIP_TONGJI_2023.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found a relevant paper on this topic:\n\n**\"Certifying MIP-Based Presolve Reductions for 0–1 Integer Linear Programs\"** appears to be a key paper in this area. The search results also reference work showing how the correctness of MIP presolve reductions on 0-1 integer linear programs can be certified by using the VeriPB tool for pseudo-Boolean proof logging, which was presented at ISMP 2024.\n\nAdditionally, experimental evaluation on both decision and optimization instances demonstrates the computational viability of the approach and leads to suggestions for future revisions of the proof format that will help to reduce the verbosity of the certificates and to accelerate the certification and verification process.\n\nThe most directly relevant result appears to be the arXiv paper from early 2024 (reference index 5), which addresses the certification of MIP presolve reductions. If you need more specific details about epsilon-optimal solutions or industrial applications in these papers, you may want to access the full arXiv paper directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"82c18b47-b659-4efe-a944-04eaa768bb5c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:27:21.496Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b4f7980e-ab58-4b67-b0c7-913130fc54e1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3515f720-e4c4-41ff-8f7a-dc7419c88f8b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a823b529b0879c4bb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFHnvc9WEFDsVJpMoxgU2c","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"learned clauses\" export import SAT solver between problems portfolio cross-instance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"HordeSat: A Massively Parallel Portfolio SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"http://ktiml.mff.cuni.cz/~balyo/papers/horde.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[1505.03340] HordeSat: A Massively Parallel Portfolio SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1505.03340\"},{\"title\":\"Revisiting Clause Exchange in Parallel SAT Solving | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-31612-8_16\"},{\"title\":\"[1908.01624] Learned Clause Minimization in Parallel SAT SolversThis work received funding from the BMBF under grant 01IH15006C (HPSV).\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1908.01624\"},{\"title\":\"SAT solver - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SAT_solver\"},{\"title\":\"IPASIR 2 Workgroup · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ipasir2\"},{\"title\":\"A Concurrent Portfolio Approach to SMT Solving\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-02658-4_60.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Ultimate SAT Solvers Handbook\",\"url\":\"https://www.numberanalytics.com/blog/sat-solvers-handbook\"},{\"title\":\"University of Potsdam Faculty of Computer Science Clause Learning in SAT\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall13/cos402/readings/SAT_learning_clauses.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about learned clauses, export/import in SAT solvers within portfolio and cross-instance contexts:\n\n## Learned Clauses in Portfolio SAT Solving\n\nThe simplest approach is to run CDCL several times on the same problem in parallel with different parameter settings and exchanging learned clauses. The solvers exchange information mainly in the form of learned clauses.\n\n## Export and Import Mechanisms\n\nThe solvers can produce and accept clauses. Not all the learned clauses are shared. It is expected that each core solver initially offers only a limited number of clauses which it considers most worthy of sharing.\n\nThe method is used to add learned clauses received from other solvers of the portfolio. The core solver can decide when and whether the clauses added using this method are actually considered during the search.\n\n## Benefits and Mechanisms\n\nExchanging learned clauses grants an additional boost of performance. It is an important mechanism to reduce duplicate work, i.e., parallel searches working on the same part of the search space.\n\nUsing efficient yet thrifty clause exchange and advanced diversification methods, solvers are able to keep the search spaces largely disjoint without explicitly splitting search spaces.\n\n## Standard Interfaces\n\nIn the typical parallel portfolio framework, SAT solvers exchange information in terms of learned clauses. Since IPASIR only supports the export of learned clauses, IPASIR-2 should also support the asynchronous import of clauses.\n\nThe research shows that clause sharing is crucial for effective portfolio-based parallel SAT solving, allowing multiple solver instances to benefit from each other's learned knowledge while working on the same problem.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8389b09f-ea8a-474d-8a12-5e7b0a17cc8b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:27:22.960Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3515f720-e4c4-41ff-8f7a-dc7419c88f8b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
